WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.789 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  finland and sweden have formally applied  to be the 31st and 32nd countries to  join nato which could allow the alliance  to fill a large gap on the map near  russia in an increasingly volatile part  of the world  but turkey a key nato member that also  sits in a strategically important  location  blocked the start of initial membership  talks for the two countries  turkey's president has said that  allowing the nordic nations to join nato  would deprive the security organization  of security his comments took western  allies by surprise and since all 30 nato  countries have to approve the addition  of any new members turkey's position has  set off a diplomatic scramble so let's  take a look at what's behind turkey's  objections and what turkey might be  seeking in order to get on board with  this historic nato expansion  first let's look at what turkey has been  saying by many measures sweden and  finland are considered some of the  world's safest countries but turkish  president recep tayyip erdogan has  called them a complete hotbed of  terrorism and he alleges that the  countries particularly sweden are  harboring terrorists who threaten  turkey's national security his main  complaint here is about the pkk or the  kurdistan workers party which has been  fighting for autonomy and is mainly  present in turkey syria iraq and iran  the pkk has been waging an insurgency  against turkey for decades and the  fighting has left tens of thousands of  people dead it has been designated a  terrorist organization not just by  turkey but by the us and the european  union though the group says it's  fighting for an autonomous kurdish  homeland and denies being a terrorist  organization so here's why this is  coming up now turkey alleges that the  governments of sweden and finland are  supporting the pkk even though those  governments technically consider it a  terrorist organization and turkey's  state-run media have been running  stories like this in recent days  alleging that swedish-made weapons have  ended up in the hands of pkk fighters  sweden which is home to thousands of  kurds with varying political views has  accused turkey of spreading  disinformation erdogan also alleges that  sweden and finland are harboring  gulenists or followers of fethullah  gulen he's a muslim cleric living in the  u.s and turkey accuses him of being  behind an attempted military coup that  stunned the world in 2016.  glenn has denied the allegations to  better understand turkey's position on  all of this i called osla aiden  toshbosch a turkish journalist turned  senior fellow at the european council on  foreign relations for a number of years  now uh turks have been feeling  entirely abandoned that they're  surrounded by enemies geographically but  also that their  allies are not their friends and this  sentiment goes back to the failed coup  attempt of 2016.  it's partly  real partly paranoia a mixture of  history and recent events but the net  result is a government that feels it has  to push back osla says that erdogan's  issue regarding these terrorists and  alleged terrorists goes beyond just  finland and sweden one particular source  of frustration is that for years the u.s  and other western nations have been  partnered with kurdish militants in  syria including an offshoot of the pkk  in order to fight isis turkey considers  these fighters to be terrorists and  after turkey launched an offensive  against kurdish fighters in syria in  2019 some european countries punished  turkey by restricting arms exports to  the country what is not clear is how  much of this is about sweden  and how much of this is about broader  set of grievances towards  allies plus osla points out that next  year is an election year in turkey and  erdogan is under immense pressure due to  turkey's faltering economy that domestic  pressure might be motivating erdogan to  secure concessions from the west  so what might those concessions be  for one turkey is pressuring finland and  sweden to crack down on any media  organizations and individuals allegedly  linked to the pkk and it's seeking the  extradition of suspected militants from  sweden erdogan also wants finland and  sweden to drop their restrictions on  arms exports to turkey beyond the pkk  issue erdogan could try to use his  leverage to get approval to buy f-16  fighter jets from the u.s and to get  turkey back into the u.s led f-35  fighter jet program the u.s kicked  turkey out of the program after turkey  bought missile systems from russia in  2019. the reality is turkish president  is not going to let this crisis go  without getting something in return  so back to finland and sweden officials  from both countries have acknowledged  turkey's concerns we take terrorism  seriously we condemn terrorism in all  its forms  and we are actively engaged in combating  it  we are right now having a dialogue with  all nato member countries including  turkey analysts say finland is mostly  caught in the crossfire when it comes to  turkey's allegations but in sweden where  there are six kurdish members of  parliament and sympathy for the kurdish  cause runs deep officials could face a  difficult balancing act in finding  common ground with turkey a big nato  summit is scheduled for the end of june  so leaders and diplomats are facing  pressure to get turkey on board and  resolve the issue before then we are  determined to work through all issues  and reach rapid conclusions despite the  holdup nato leaders say they expect  turkey to come around on voting yes to a  historic nato expansion an expansion  prompted by russia's war in ukraine to  learn more about how putin's war has  strengthened nato in other ways check  out this video thanks for watching  [Music]

